  "self_critique": "Overall assessment\n\nThe run found a small improvement\u20140.602988 versus roughly 0.6018 baseline, about +0.0012\u2014but the evidence is too thin to call the search converged or the gain robust.\n\nWhat the harness/policy did suboptimally\n\n- Stopped too early. \u201cConverged\u201d after only one successful improvement, one proposal failure, and one rejected ensemble is not well supported.\n- Bundled too many changes in node_001: architecture, BCE/BPR mixing, dropout, AdamW, step decay, and recency weighting. This prevents attribution and makes subsequent tuning inefficient.\n- Overstated the hypothesis. The predicted +0.0029 improvement was more than twice the realized gain.\n- Continued from a failed node. node_003 is recorded as descending from node_002 even though it uses node_001\u2019s model. Failed nodes should not become executable parents.\n- Jumped to ensembling prematurely. The gain from node_001 had not first been reproduced across seeds. A three-seed ensemble is expensive and can degrade rank metrics if averaging/calibration is inappropriate.\n- Lacked uncertainty reporting. A baseline sigma is shown, but there is no comparable multi-seed variance or paired confidence interval for node_001. A +0.0012 gain may be noise.\n- Logged node_002 only as \u201cproposal failed,\u201d leaving no actionable diagnosis.\n- Did not investigate why the ensemble fell to baseline: individual seed scores, aggregation method, checkpoint selection, and prediction alignment should have been recorded.\n\nWhat I would change in the scaffold\n\n- Require valid executable ancestry; on proposal failure, retry from the last accepted node.\n- Separate compound proposals into staged ablations, changing one major factor at a time.\n- Require two or three seed reproductions before accepting a small gain as established.\n- Use paired evaluation and confidence intervals, not only point estimates.\n- Record full failure diagnostics and all individual ensemble-member metrics.\n- Add a minimum evidence rule for convergence: several local perturbations with no improvement, not merely a failed proposal and a rejected ensemble.\n- Calibrate expected effect sizes from prior observed deltas rather than optimistic literature-based estimates.\n- Preserve exact configs and checkpoints so accepted nodes can be reproduced independently.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n\nFirst, reproduce node_001 exactly across three independent seeds without ensembling. Compare each seed and the mean against a similarly seeded baseline using paired evaluation. If the gain survives, ablate the node_001 package in this order:\n\n1. Recency weighting.\n2. BCE/BPR mixing coefficient around 0.5.\n3. DCN-lite versus baseline FM.\n4. Dropout/AdamW settings.\n5. Step-decay schedule.\n\nThen locally tune only the components shown to matter. Do not retry ensembling until individual models consistently exceed baseline; if ensembling is revisited, compare score averaging, rank averaging, and validation-optimized weights while reporting every member\u2019s standalone score."
}
